      "paperId": "0d7ddddeaf93799ffbe9cd78df37cdf5db04275a",
      "title": "THEODICY IN ANCIENT PHILOSOPHY: FROM PRESOCRATICS TO PHILO",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Vladyslav Riazanov",
          "authorId": "2398499516"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "The problem of theodicy is one of the oldest and most complex issues in Christian thought. Genetically, it originates from a theistic worldview model, which, on the one hand, determined the conceptual framework for its resolution and, on the other hand, delineated the boundaries of its possible interpretations. At the same time, the problem of theodicy did not emerge exclusively within the framework of Christian discourse. Its origins can be traced back to ancient culture and philosophy, where fundamental ideas concerning the nature of evil, divine justice, and human moral responsibility had already begun to take shape. Accordingly, this article provides a historical-philosophical analysis of the formation and development of the problem of theodicy in ancient philosophy, from the Presocratic period to the teachings of Philo of Alexandria. The study examines the genesis of ideas about the nature of evil, the relationship between the divine principle and human moral responsibility, as well as the formation of the conceptual foundations of later Christian theodicy. It is argued that although classical theodicy is traditionally associated with medieval Christian thought, its central ideas emerged much earlier within the context of ancient philosophical tradition. The article analyzes the interpretation of evil in the thought of the Presocratics, particularly Heraclitus and Democritus, who associated evil with the imperfection of human nature, ignorance, or the disruption of cosmic order. It is demonstrated that the problem of theodicy attained its most systematic form in Plato\u2019s philosophy, where God and the Good are identified and the possibility of divine responsibility for evil is rejected. Particular attention is devoted to Plato\u2019s dialogues Republic, Timaeus, Theaetetus, and Laws, in which he established the foundations of the later European discourse on theodicy. The study also examines the contributions of Euclid and Aristotle to the development of the conception of evil as non-being or as a self-destructive phenomenon. Special emphasis is placed on the thought of Philo of Alexandria, who integrated Platonic cosmology with the biblical tradition and laid the groundwork for the patristic model of theodicy. The article concludes that ancient philosophy constituted a significant intellectual foundation for the subsequent development of Christian philosophy of religion and classical conceptions of the justification of God in the face of the existence of evil.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.15421/342610",
      "arxivId": null,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0d7ddddeaf93799ffbe9cd78df37cdf5db04275a",
      "venue": "Epistemological Studies in Philosophy Social and Political Sciences"
    },
    {
      "paperId": "becdbbfa24663f11ee778020831cabb1180ba766",
      "title": "The Romantic Cosmic Meaning of Life",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Peter K\u00fcgler",
          "authorId": "102133702"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "Abstract The idea of a cosmic meaning of life refers to an evaluation of human life as part of the world. It does not require a view of the world \u201cfrom nowhere within it\u201d, nor does it involve a search for some cosmic purpose. While meaning and purpose were often conflated in later philosophy, they were separate (yet linked) in early German Romanticism, where the concept of the meaning of life originated. The Romantics saw the cosmic meaning of life in the fact that we belong to a world that is beautiful and sublime, and that evokes attitudes of awe and reverence. In this paper it is argued that a romantic conception of meaning is compatible with both subjectivism and objectivism, but that the subjective component \u2013 the experience of meaning \u2013 must be present. While the traditional source of cosmic meaning is religion, the example of John Dewey shows that a romantic worldview can appeal to naturalists as well. When we see ourselves as part of the world, we realise that we need to cooperate with nature. This could mean that nature is used as an instrument for human purposes or that it is considered to be intrinsically valuable.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.1515/humaff-2025-0095",
      "arxivId": null,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/becdbbfa24663f11ee778020831cabb1180ba766",
      "venue": "Human Affairs"
    },
    {
      "paperId": "9ad3a19c10e934d2371d3a488775ad7fb23a3fde",
      "title": "Belief, No-Fault Unbelief and Medieval Christian Thought",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Roberto Di Ceglie",
          "authorId": "147894667"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "Traditionally, Christians have viewed unbelief as resulting from moral and/or cognitive shortcomings. However, a recent proposal such as John Schellenberg's 'divine hiddenness argument' for atheism has supported the idea that there is no-fault unbelief. In this Element, the author draws on important insights from medieval Christian thought to argue that both believers and non-believers should stop blaming each other based on mere evidence. Believers may recognise that no-fault unbelief can, in a sense, apply to them, too. They may find it perplexing that they neither believe nor achieve communion with God as much as they would like. Proponents of no-fault unbelief might focus on achieving communion with God rather than solely worrying about absolving themselves of blame. Ultimately, believers and non-believers alike might promote spiritual progress by setting aside the primacy of evidence and committing themselves to God and the good.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.1017/9781009790482",
      "arxivId": null,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/9ad3a19c10e934d2371d3a488775ad7fb23a3fde",
      "venue": ""
    },
    {
      "paperId": "7a28b4f44572531d7f06c8d584a6a7ba87bd3785",
      "title": "On the ineffability and the noetic quality of mystical experiences",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Gabriel Mart\u00ednez Villarreal",
          "authorId": "2434372549"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "According to William James, mystical states of consciousness are characterized, among other features, by their noetic quality, i. e., some knowledge obtained is through them. However, another common feature of mystical experiences is their ineffability. This raises an epistemological question: How can something be known, and yet cannot be expressed? Through the conceptual framework of Ludwig Wittgenstein\u2019s Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus, we examine mystical experiences as sources of knowledge. In Wittgenstein\u2019s conception, the mystical is placed at a liminal and negative position: it lies outside the limits of language and of the world, and it refers to that which cannot be said. Despite the weak and problematic epistemic foundation of these experiences from Wittgenstein\u2019s early perspective, we conclude with some reflections on their pragmatic and subjective value for the religious dimension of human life.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.57027/eikasia.136.1010",
      "arxivId": null,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/7a28b4f44572531d7f06c8d584a6a7ba87bd3785",
      "venue": "Eikasia. Revista de filosof\u00eda"
    },
    {
      "paperId": "e5247697c6d40540148a92698a657fdb65eac54e",
      "title": "The Love of God? Bhakti (Devotion) and the Virtues in Spinoza\u2019s Ethics (Parts IV and V) and Bhagavadg\u012bt\u0101 Chapters 12\u201314 (Bhaktiyoga)",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Lisa Widdison",
          "authorId": "147384194"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "Common sense tells us that feeling love involves loving another as another and is not merely an accident of self-love, while the Sanskrit theory of rasa aesthetics tells us that genuine love must be returned. God\u2019s love for humanity would not require these distinctions, however, if it exists at all, and Benedictus de Spinoza (1632\u20131677) claims that it does not. Rather, he finds that God\u2019s love is not a philosophical problem because the very idea of God experiencing pleasure or pain as a result of desire for another (which constitutes common transactional conceptions of love) is irrational. This philosophical problem is compounded by the intrinsic value of loving without reciprocity, the follies of delusion, and the complicated\u2014if not implicit\u2014demands of reciprocity. Although Spinoza teaches a devotional path to liberation based on a logic of emotion in his Ethics, it is in the Bhagavadg\u012bt\u0101\u2019s twenty verses on \u201cBhaktiyoga\u201d that a philosophy of devotion extends to a practice for the sake of love in moral action. This virtue-theoretic approach to emotion responses yields yoga-classed results such that the characteristic traits of love are dedicated to humanity and productive actions are offered to God. This study reconciles the complex challenge of achieving adequate moral knowledge with Spinoza\u2019s claims that the path is rare, not difficult. If knowledge of what to do can be united with how to serve, divine love may be theoretically realised. The conclusion is that one may conduct ordinary secular transactions without contradiction yet generate a kind of affective currency as a channel for experiencing embodied liberation in a virtuous friendship with humanity via God.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.3390/rel17050588",
      "arxivId": null,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e5247697c6d40540148a92698a657fdb65eac54e",
      "venue": "Religions"
    },
    {
      "paperId": "3cbcecd6a0d375d681d193a306c104c9ae97feaa",
      "title": "A Glimpse into Liturgical Theology",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "D\u00e1vid Di\u00f3si",
          "authorId": "2439735176"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "What is the liturgy, and what does it reveal about the God who acts within it? This volume approaches Christian worship not as a mere external rite or practical discipline but as a multidimensional theological reality: a living encounter between the God who seeks man and the man who is capable of receiving Him. Drawing on the history of religions, philosophy, sociology, and systematic theology, the author explores the liturgy as a cultic site, a communication event, a participation in divine life, a sanctification of God\u2019s name, and an anticipation of creation\u2019s eschatological fulfilment. In conscious dialogue with the challenges of postmodern culture, the book rehabilitates the often-neglected concepts of cult, the sacred, and symbolic mediation, while insisting on the theocentric character of worship. The result is a work of liturgical theology that seeks the substance behind the visible signs: the radiance of the living God shining through the rites and prayers of His Church.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.56037/978-963-646-598-8",
      "arxivId": null,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3cbcecd6a0d375d681d193a306c104c9ae97feaa",
      "venue": "Babe\u0219-Bolyai University Series. Theological Studies"
