      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "The study aims to reveal conceptual differences in the interpretations of the secular in the works of G. Vahanian and T. J. J. Altizer, key representatives of the radical theology of the 1960s. The article analyzes the cultural, philosophical, and theological prerequisites of Death of God theology, examines the interpretations of the secular by both authors, and provides a comparison based on the ontological status of the \u201cdeath of God\u201d formula, the understanding of the secular as a mode of relating God, the world, and man, and the attitude towards religious tradition. Special attention is paid to the distinction between secularity and secularism in Vahanian\u2019s thought and the onto-kenotic interpretation of immanence in Altizer\u2019s work. The scientific novelty lies in constructing an internal typology of radical theology, which conceptually differentiates between the cultural-critical and onto-kenotic models of understanding the secular, which differ in their principle of correlating transcendence and immanence rather than in their degree of radicalism. As a result, it is established that for Vahanian, the secular acts as a desacralized space of responsibility and a condition for purifying faith from religious idols, whereas for Altizer, it appears as a consequence of the irreversible kenosis of the transcendent God in the Incarnation; thus, the \u201cdeath of God\u201d formula functions in two conceptual modes \u2013 as a cultural-hermeneutic diagnosis and as an ontological event.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.30853/mns20260099",
      "arxivId": null,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f09badf5e86e39e5546d5180024e172e3cfb97ff",
      "venue": "Manuscript"
