    {
      "paperId": "f362dd626b9fe5d22ccf77d84696d04ecc8fcad8",
      "title": "Personality Development in V. Nesmelov\u2019s Religious and Philosophical Anthropology",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "I. Druzhkin",
          "authorId": "2440853780"
        },
        {
          "name": "M. Antipov",
          "authorId": "2188106603"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "The article is devoted to the religious and philosophical concept of man, formed in the works by V. Nesmelov. The relevance of the work is stipulated by the fact that V. Nesmelov was one of the first to lay the foundation for the existentialist trend in Russian religious philosophy, as well as the significance of his views on man for modern philosophy of education and Christian pedagogy. The subject of the research is the pedagogical potential of the thinker\u2019s philosophical anthropology. The concept constructed in his \u201cScience of Man\u201d can serve as a methodological basis for the theory and practice of education as a process of formation of spiritual and moral qualities in a person. Methodology and methods: theological approach, existentialist approach, comparative method. The novelty of the paper consists in identifying the pedagogical content in the religious and philosophical views of the Kazan thinker. Results: the origins of V. Nesmelov\u2019s religious and philosophical anthropology are considered, V. Nesmelov\u2019s idea of man as a free, reasonable and self-aware person capable of spiritualizing oneself and the world is reconstructed; the philosopher\u2019s views on the problems of theodicy and soteriology are revealed. It is found out that the education based on the science of man by V. Nesmelov should be aimed at forming better awareness of oneself and one\u2019s place in the world, given by God.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.64884/3034-2732-2026-2-5",
      "arxivId": null,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f362dd626b9fe5d22ccf77d84696d04ecc8fcad8",
      "venue": "Bulletin of Penza Theological Seminary"
    },
    {
      "paperId": "ae7e18e67d1094c96c6f05afcdf24a5a5a80a32d",
      "title": "Imagining God",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Kathryn A. Johnson",
          "authorId": "2358467842"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "Introduces the topic of God representations in monotheistic traditions. Section 2 examines belief in the authoritarian (e.g., controlling and punishing) and benevolent (e.g., helping and forgiving) attributes of God as a person-like being. The discussion is expanded in Section 3 to include abstract representations (e.g., the Universe, Nature, and negative theology). Section 4 describes measures used to assess people's beliefs about God and presents survey data of group differences in beliefs about God as authoritarian and benevolent. Section 5 addresses the under-studied question: where is God? Representations of God do not exist in a vacuum, and Section 6 explores the cognitive building blocks, life circumstances, worldviews, and personal motivations that can inform diverse God representations. Finally, Section 7 concludes with an overview of some of the antecedents and outcomes of God representations surveyed in this Element and how they relate to various ways of thinking about, relating to, and imagining God.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.1017/9781009527842",
      "arxivId": null,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ae7e18e67d1094c96c6f05afcdf24a5a5a80a32d",
      "venue": ""
    },
    {
      "paperId": "4f0fb6cfea4e37acb649164ae942e8f81004911f",
      "title": "Kant: Der Staat, die Religion und das Religionsverfassungsrecht",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Peter Unruh",
          "authorId": "50190150"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2025,
      "abstract": "The examination of Kants view on the state-church-law needs first to uncover the philosophical foundations of his philosophical system. So a reconstruction of Kants autonomy-based moral philosophy is followed by an analysis of his legal and political philosophy including the concept of law and the basic terms of dignity of men, political freedom and legal equity. An overview of Kants philosophy of religion leeds to some key elements of his theory of state-church-law, especially the freedom of religion and the autonomy of religious communities. The conclusion flows into an ambivalent assessment of the mentioned parts of Kants thinking.",
