    {
      "paperId": "599e9b8ffd7ec9684b2a1b99b697c185fb1377e0",
      "title": "\u201cIn emulation opposite to Heaven\u201d",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "\u00c1gnes Bat\u00f3",
          "authorId": "2078290198"
        }
      ],
      "year": null,
      "abstract": "Present dissertation focuses on the phenomenon of imitation and its representation in John Milton\u2019s Paradise Lost (1667). The patterns of imitation, based on the theories of Gabriel Tarde (1962) and Ren\u00e9 Girard (1978) govern the providential narrative as well as account for the rebellion and the fall of both angels and men. Furthermore, imitation is the backdrop of two fundamental metaphors, kinship and \u201cimage\u201d, which constitute an interpretative scaffolding throughout the work. Both metaphors operate on a mimetic basis, and they convey the divine will, the reunion with God and the elevation of humankind. At the same time, these metaphors are deeply embedded in human experience, and the fallen connotations evoke the dangerous, rivalistic side of imitation. The fatherhood of Satan, as opposed to the fatherhood of God demonstrates the contradiction between the mimetic nature of creation and the autonomy of created beings, while Eve\u2019s experience with images reflects the insecurities surrounding the human condition. However, by declaring the Son of God to rule as Adams Son (3.286), mankind receives the promise to be dignified and exalted.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.14232/phd.12892",
      "arxivId": null,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/599e9b8ffd7ec9684b2a1b99b697c185fb1377e0",
      "venue": ""
    },
    {
      "paperId": "a9bafca67f04de6b33bbab0cb8d6142ec5a22d66",
      "title": "Conceptualising God in Nature: Empirically Testing Conceptions of God-Nature Overlap in Dialogue with Christian Ecotheology",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "T. Tanton",
          "authorId": "2299900806"
        },
        {
          "name": "Arian Rajaeian",
          "authorId": "2440039496"
        },
        {
          "name": "Adam Baimel",
          "authorId": "1490951318"
        },
        {
          "name": "Kathryn A. Johnson",
          "authorId": "2358467842"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "Christian theology has been critiqued from an ecological perspective for desacralising the natural world. This desacralisation, it is proposed, leads to a conceptual separation between God and nature, which ultimately devalues the natural world, leading to exploitative environmental attitudes. In response, some Christian ecotheologians have advocated for the notion that God permeates the natural world. In keeping with the \u2018Greening of Religion\u2019 hypothesis, we provide empirical scrutiny of the intuition that conceptions of God/Nature overlap relate to environmentalism. In two studies, we found only two significant correlations between differing conceptualisations of God/Nature overlap and several published scales on pro-environmental attitudes and intentions. This null result calls into question the long-standing notion that conceptualising God as overlapping with Nature inevitably leads to pro-environmentalism. Instead, consistent with previous literature, we conclude that conceptualising the Self as psychologically close to Nature is a far more robust positive predictor of environmentalism.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.1177/00916471261448590",
      "arxivId": null,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/a9bafca67f04de6b33bbab0cb8d6142ec5a22d66",
      "venue": "Journal of psychology and theology"
    },
    {
      "paperId": "4dba6ed187369f47c8159122cf9e8ac1ec690900",
      "title": "Key Theological Themes in the Vatican II's Dogmatic Constitution Dei Verbum [On Divine Revelation]: Part 2",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "J. Sullivan",
          "authorId": "2070450633"
        },
        {
          "name": "P. McPartlan",
          "authorId": "113960866"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "Abstract:In this interview, Monsignor Paul McPartlan reflects on the key theological and p Knights of Columbusastoral themes of Vatican II's Dogmatic Constitution Dei Verbum, emphasizing its continued relevance for the Church today. He underscores the inseparable unity of Sacred Scripture and Tradition as a single deposit of faith entrusted to the entire people of God, highlighting the collaborative role of both the Magisterium and the faithful. Monsignor McPartlan draws connections between Dei Verbum and Lumen Gentium, particularly the prioritization of the whole Church before the hierarchy and the importance of the sensus fidei. He also explores the pastoral dimension, describing divine revelation as a personal, dialogical invitation from God. The conversation situates the document as foundational for the Church's synodal journey and deepening faith.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.1353/jec.2024.a988558",
      "arxivId": null,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/4dba6ed187369f47c8159122cf9e8ac1ec690900",
