      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "In this paper, the central theological issue of Paradise Lost that John Milton addresses is given a decolonial reinterpretation\nin terms of the indigenous ecological ethics of Chotanagpur. The work has traditionally been interpreted in a EuroChristian context, which accentuates the themes of sin, disobedience, and divine justice, and usually limits Milton to\nlegalistic concepts of morality in his epic. The paper calls into question those limited interpretations by putting the ideas\nof Milton of free will and divine providence into conversation with Adivasi cosmologies, which are based on the ethical\nprinciple of relationality, ecological balance, and communal responsibility. Based on postcolonial theory, ecocriticism,\nand indigenous epistemology, the paper asserts that moral failure, in Chotanagpur tribal thought, is not perceived as\npersonal sin, but as the loss of cosmic harmony that includes land, ancestors and community. By conducting a critical\nanalysis of the important scenes in Paradise Lost, especially the account of the fall, this paper will show that Milton's\nParadise Lost can be redefined as an ecological system and not a mere symbolic or theological system. The paper suggests\nthat theodicy can be developed as a non-punitive, restorative model by focusing on relational accountability rather than\njuridical guilt by pointing out the ethical framework in Chotanagpur. This way, it adds to the current attempts to decolonise\nboth literary and theological discourse by legitimising indigenous knowledge systems as critical systems. Finally, the\nresearch contributes to the Miltonic literature by locating the significance of the book in a global ethical discourse and the\nfact that it remains relevant in modern argumentation about ecological justice, moral responsibility, and cultural plurality.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.25258/ijddt.16.30s.119",
      "arxivId": null,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c7ad717d7eb82546b6e8eb08a0bada1007829298",
      "venue": "International Journal of Drug Delivery Technology"
    },
    {
      "paperId": "f6729ab2282e874f7b92cdb4b17cea8ae41dff95",
      "title": "Jesus Christ: The Eternal Son, True God and True Man, Crucified Savior, Risen Lord, and Coming King",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "S. Sangwa",
          "authorId": "2400300090"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "This chapter presents the doctrine of Jesus Christ as the living center of Christian faith, worship, salvation, mission, discipleship, and spiritual discernment. Scripture reveals Jesus Christ as the eternal Son of God, the Word who was with God and was God, and the Word who became flesh for the salvation of sinners. He is true God and true man, conceived by the Holy Spirit, born of the virgin Mary, without sin, obedient to the Father, crucified under human injustice and divine purpose, raised bodily from the dead, ascended to the right hand of the Father, and coming again in glory to judge the living and the dead. The chapter argues from Scripture alone that Christ is not merely a prophet, moral teacher, religious reformer, created being, angelic mediator, spiritual master, symbolic myth, or one path among many. He is the only Mediator between God and humanity, the Lamb of God who takes away sin, the risen Lord, and the coming King. The chapter develops a biblical Christology by tracing the promise of Christ in the Old Testament, His incarnation and public ministry in the Gospels, His saving work interpreted by the apostles, and His final victory revealed in the hope of His return. It explains the deity of Christ, the humanity of Christ, the hypostatic union, His sinless obedience, substitutionary atonement, bodily resurrection, ascension, intercession, lordship, exclusivity, and second coming. It also addresses historical errors and contemporary distortions, including Arianism, adoptionism, Docetism, Gnosticism, Ebionism, Apollinarianism, Nestorian distortion, Eutychian confusion, Socinianism, Unitarianism, modalistic confusion, liberal demythologizing, progressive reductions of Christ, prosperity distortions, universalism, religious pluralism, Christ consciousness, Cosmic Christ theories, ascended master teachings, channeling, self-deification, and technological messianism. The chapter concludes with pastoral application, reflection questions, and assessment materials for Bible College formation.",
