    {
      "paperId": "1dbde60d4c44fb2a13695b6c2f00a4f6b74b9872",
      "title": "Aliens, Demons, and the Coming Deception: A Biblical-Theological, Historical, and Phenomenological Reassessment of the Extraterrestrial Narrative",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "S. Sangwa",
          "authorId": "2400300090"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "This article argues that a significant portion of modern alien discourse is better interpreted through biblical categories of deceptive spiritual agency than through the publicly unverified assumption of extraterrestrial visitation. The argument is cumulative rather than monocausal. It does not claim that every unidentified aerial or anomalous report is demonic, nor that every abduction account is a literal physical kidnapping by non-human beings. Rather, it proceeds in five steps. First, it clarifies the article\u2019s epistemic posture and distinguishes biblical theology, public institutional reporting, phenomenology, and eschatological inference. Second, it develops a biblical theology of counterfeit supernaturalism, rival revelation, and end-time delusion. Third, it incorporates primary esoteric sources, especially Alice A. Bailey\u2019s language of the \u201cexternalisation of the hierarchy\u201d and Benjamin Creme\u2019s presentation of UFOs as spiritually guided collaborators of Maitreya, to show that modern alien discourse often functions as an overtly religious and messianic system. Fourth, it re-examines abduction claims in light of sleep paralysis research, cross-cultural nocturnal assault traditions, and the recurring pattern of fear, paralysis, telepathy, domination, and anti-biblical revelation. Fifth, within a dispensational pre-tribulational framework, it considers how the alien narrative could serve as a post-rapture explanatory myth able to absorb social shock while redirecting humanity away from divine judgment. The article concludes that Christians should reject both credulous extraterrestrial speculation and careless sensationalism, recover biblical discernment, and recognize the alien question as part of a larger conflict over revelation, worship, and truth.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.65655/openchristianpress.2026.107",
      "arxivId": null,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1dbde60d4c44fb2a13695b6c2f00a4f6b74b9872",
      "venue": "Open Journal of Science, Philosophy &amp; Theology (ISSN: 3105-3041)"
    },
    {
      "paperId": "3d918462e26b7a6a6acb3e361aab82ec509d4710",
      "title": "BIBLICAL \u00ab\u05d1\u05bc\u05b8\u05e8\u05d0\u05b8\u00bb (BARA) IN THE CONTEXT OF CHRISTIAN CREATIONISM",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Nazarii Malaniak",
          "authorId": "2433388076"
