    {
      "paperId": "0019963657ac0e0957c574494255b62860e58247",
      "title": "\u2018Active and Affirmative Gods\u2019: Polytheism in\n Nietzsche and Philosophy",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Grant Maxwell",
          "authorId": "144579084"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "What does Gilles Deleuze mean by his equation of forces and gods in Nietzsche and Philosophy? Are the gods metaphorical figures for the forces central to this text? Are they real divinities to whom one might pray or sacrifice? Or is the ontological status of these gods more ambiguous and paradoxical? This article takes seriously Deleuze's equation, exploring its implications for the Nietzschean\u2013Deleuzian theory of forces. For Deleuze reading Nietzsche, the one that wants in the will is the relationality of gods, and the will to power, embodied in Dionysus, is a will towards relational complexes of divine potencies luring becoming.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.3366/dlgs.2026.0635",
      "arxivId": null,
